          body: "AI images in 2026 are marked in up to three ways at once: C2PA Content Credentials and [EXIF](/blog/what-is-exif-data)/XMP/IPTC tags, which are metadata, and SynthID, which is an invisible watermark baked into the pixels. The difference that matters is where each lives: metadata sits in the file container and is easily stripped by screenshots, social uploads, and metadata tools, while a pixel watermark survives all of those. So “can I remove the AI marking?” has two answers: the metadata layer (C2PA, EXIF, IPTC, XMP) is removable with a client-side tool like MetaStrip; a SynthID pixel watermark is not, because no metadata tool can reach it. Leading AI tools now layer all three precisely so each covers the others’ weakness.",
