          heading: "FAQ",
          body: "**Is C2PA now an official standard?** Yes. C2PA Content Credentials graduated from an industry coalition specification to a formal ISO standard and are now C2PA 2.1, ratified as ISO/IEC 22144. That gives it legal and procurement weight, which is why membership has grown past 6,000 organizations including Google, Meta, OpenAI, Adobe, Sony, and Nikon.\n\n**Do all AI image generators add provenance data now?** As of 2026, effectively yes: Google Gemini and Imagen, Adobe Firefly, OpenAI’s DALL-E and ChatGPT, and Midjourney all embed C2PA content credentials by default. Many cameras (Sony, Nikon, Leica, Canon, Samsung, Pixel) now sign photos at capture too, so the absence of credentials is itself becoming a signal.\n\n**When does the EU AI Act require AI content to be labeled?** Article 50’s transparency obligations begin enforcement on August 2, 2026. They require providers of AI systems that generate synthetic images, audio, video, or text to mark that content in a machine-readable format, and C2PA is the EU’s preferred mechanism. California’s SB 942 already took effect on January 1, 2026.\n\n**Can I remove C2PA and AI metadata from my images?** Yes, the metadata layer (C2PA manifests, XMP AI fields, IPTC markers, EXIF generation parameters) is removable client-side with a tool like MetaStrip. It won’t remove pixel-level watermarks like SynthID, and removing disclosure from AI content that regulations require to be labeled may carry legal implications depending on where the content appears.\n\n**Does removing metadata stop platforms from detecting AI images?** It defeats the metadata-based detection layer, which is the most common and easily checked signal, so it’s effective against many automated systems. But it doesn’t remove durable pixel-level watermarks like SynthID or help against machine-learning classifiers, so it isn’t a guarantee of non-detection.",
